## Summary of Coordinate System

when rect object is created.
```
rect = new fabric.Rect({
  originX, originY,
  left, top, width, height,
  scaleX, scaleY, flipX, flipY, angle, skewX, skewY,
  padding, strokeWidth,
})
```

`originX` and `originY` determine the reference point of an object relative to its parent's coordinate system (either a group or the canvas). These properties define how the object's position is anchored within its parent container.

`left` and `top` represent the position of the object's anchor point relative to its parent container. These values are expressed in the local coordinate system of the parent.

`width` and `height` represent the object's dimensions in its local coordinate system. These values define the original size of the object before any transformations such as scaling, flipping, rotation, or skewing are applied.
Additionally, these values do not include the strokeWidth. Therefore, to calculate the actual visible size of an object, you must add the strokeWidth to the width and height values.

To get the actual visible size of an object, use `object.getBoundingRect(true)`. This function returns the object's dimensions after all transformations (scaling, shifting, flipping, skewing and rotation) have been applied and includes the strokeWidth in its calculations. The returned values represent the bounding rectangle in the local coordinate system, without applying the canvas's viewportTransform.

To transform coordinates between different coordinate systems:

- Use `object.calcOwnMatrix()` to get the transformation matrix that converts coordinates from the object's local coordinate system to its parent container's coordinate system.

- Use `object.calcTransformMatrix()` to get the transformation matrix that converts coordinates from the object's local coordinate system all the way to the canvas coordinate system. This includes all transformations applied by parent groups.

These matrices can be used to convert point coordinates between different coordinate systems by multiplying the point coordinates with the appropriate matrix.

Therefore, `object.calcTransformMatrix()` is equivalent to the multiplication of transformation matrices from all parent groups: `parent of parent group.calcOwnMatrix()` × ... × `parent group.calcOwnMatrix()` × `object.calcOwnMatrix()`. Applying this combined transformation matrix to a position coordinate produces the same result as applying each individual matrix in sequence.
